WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.280
A sustainable CMMC program begins with governance.

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:06.720
The organization needs accountable leaders, defined roles, approved policy,

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:10.155
decision authority, issue escalation, supplier expectations,

00:00:10.155 --> 00:00:13.060
and a way to track changing contract obligations.

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:16.030
Contract analysis and information discovery come before tool selection.

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:21.775
Teams should understand applicable clauses, required CMMC status, FCI and CUI categories,

00:00:21.775 --> 00:00:26.680
data flows, recipients, locations, external services, and subcontractor relationships.

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:29.080
A clear system boundary reduces uncertainty.

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:33.960
Inventories, diagrams, ownership, approved applications, security-protection assets,

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:37.555
and external providers help the organization explain which environments are covered and

00:00:37.555 --> 00:00:41.600
why. Gap analysis compares current implementation with applicable requirements.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:46.000
Findings need accountable owners, realistic milestones, resources, risk visibility,

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:46.829
and verification.

00:00:46.829 --> 00:00:50.890
A policy statement alone does not resolve a technical or operational gap.

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:52.365
Documentation should match reality.

00:00:52.365 --> 00:00:57.240
The System Security Plan, policies, procedures, inventories, diagrams, tickets, logs,

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:58.020
training records,

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:01.595
and supplier records should describe and demonstrate the way the environment actually

00:01:01.595 --> 00:01:04.910
operates. Supplier governance is part of the program.

00:01:04.910 --> 00:01:07.400
Organizations need processes to identify flow-down requirements,

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:11.700
communicate handling expectations, review provider responsibilities, track status,

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:16.195
and avoid sharing protected information before approved safeguards are in place.

00:01:16.195 --> 00:01:18.585
Continuous monitoring helps maintain the assessed condition.

00:01:18.585 --> 00:01:22.690
Access reviews, configuration checks, vulnerability management, incident response,

00:01:22.690 --> 00:01:25.060
change management, evidence review, training,

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:29.240
and internal assessments can detect drift before it becomes a larger gap.

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:30.450
Metrics should support decisions.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:35.515
Leaders need visibility into scope coverage, unresolved requirements, overdue remediation,

00:01:35.515 --> 00:01:38.840
supplier risk, training completion, evidence health, exceptions, incidents,

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:40.720
and changes that could affect status.

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:44.540
The most effective programs treat CMMC as part of normal contract performance and

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:49.260
cybersecurity operations. Controls become routine work, evidence is produced naturally,

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:52.440
and compliance supports protection rather than competing with it.

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:57.250
CMMC 2.0 helps the Department of Defense verify safeguards for FCI and CUI across the

00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:58.945
Defense Industrial Base.

00:01:58.945 --> 00:02:02.785
Success depends on current official requirements, disciplined implementation,

00:02:02.785 --> 00:02:04.800
honest evidence, and continuous improvement.
